 ## Overview
-This method automates the design of volume rendering transfer functions (TF) by clustering material boundaries defined in the Lower-Higher (LH) intensity space, a 2D domain where boundaries appear as distinct blobs rather than overlapping arches [S1]. It solves the problem of manual TF specification by identifying initial clustering elements from histogram peaks, grouping them via hierarchical clustering (using Bhattacharyya similarity) or mean-shift clustering, and mapping the resulting clusters to color and opacity [S1][S2, unofficial]. It sets itself apart from standard scalar-gradient clustering by utilizing the LH transformation to simplify cluster shapes and reduce classification ambiguities [S1], and from pure statistical methods by incorporating spatial connectivity and boundary path integration [S2, unofficial].
+This method automates the design of volume rendering transfer functions (TF) by clustering material boundaries defined in the Lightness-Hue (LH) space, a 2D histogram domain where boundaries appear as distinct blobs rather than overlapping arches [S1][S2, unofficial]. It solves the problem of manual TF specification by identifying initial clustering elements from histogram peaks and grouping them via hierarchical clustering, allowing users to interact with cluster hierarchies without predefined thresholds [S1]. Unlike standard scalar-value vs. gradient-magnitude spaces, the LH space simplifies cluster shapes and reduces classification ambiguities [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-The method operates in two primary variants described in the sources: a hierarchical approach based on peak detection and Bhattacharyya similarity [S1], and a mean-shift approach based on gradient integration and convex hulls [S2, unofficial]. Both variants share the goal of mapping volumetric data to visual properties by clustering voxels based on their scalar values and spatial locations to reduce occlusions [S2, unofficial].
+The method operates in two primary variants: the original hierarchical clustering approach using Bhattacharyya similarity [S1], and a variant using Mean Shift clustering followed by region growing [S2, unofficial].
 
